#### \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm lead}\) cut

As discussed earlier in Sec. 6.2, the purely combinatorial jets present in the \(p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm reco,ch}\) distribution need to be suppressed. The above mentioned cut on the jet area is a good step, however not a sufficient one. A severe cut on the momentum of jet constituents would be efficient, on the other hand this would impose a serious bias on the jet fragmentation and make the jet quenching studies extremely complicated. A good compromise is to impose a cut on the \(p_{\rm T}\) of jet's leading hadron (\(p_{\rm T}^{\rm lead}\)). This cut reduces significantly the number of soft (combinatorial) jets on one side while, on the other side, still keeps large number of jets containing mainly soft hadrons in the jet population, which are important for jet quenching studies. The trade off is that the reconstruction algorithm is no more 100% collinear safe.

The size of the effect of the \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm lead}\) cut on final results has to be carefully studied. On Fig. 6.13 one can see the effect on the uncorrected spectra. Clearly, the LHS which is dominated by the background is suppressed much strongly than the right hand side (RHS), which is dominated by the signal. However one has to compare the fully corrected spectra with different \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm lead}\) cut values in order to draw any physics conclusions.

