### 6.4 Correction for Instrumental Effects

Generally, instrumental response, \(R_{\rm instr}\), can be measured utilizing the PYTHIA event generator and GEANT simulations of particle transport and real-time detector conditions. Unfortunatelly, in our case the full GEANT simulation of the entire detector was not available, therefore we made a parametrization of the key detector performance variables, namely TPC tracking efficiency and TPC momentum resolution. These effects were already studied in detail and described in Sec. 6.1.1 and 6.1.2.

The effect of the imperfect tracking efficiency was simulated in PYTHIA 6 by applying the efficiency cut on every jet constituent - random number \(x\subset(0,1)\) is generated and if \(x>\epsilon_{h}\), the track is discarded.

The effect of the finite TPC resolution was simulated in PYTHIA by randomly smearing \(p_{\rm T}\) of each charged track as

\[p_{\rm T}^{smeared}={\rm N}(\mu,\sigma)={\rm N}(p_{\rm T},\sigma(p_{\rm T})) \tag{6.8}\]

#### Instrumental Response

Instrumental response matrix \(R_{\rm instr}\)=\({\bf R}\left[p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm part}\to p_{\rm T,jet}^{\rm dete}\right]\) is obtained by comparing simulated particle-level and detector-level jets. We proceeded in several steps:

* A parton is generated with a uniform \(p_{\rm T}\) from 0 to 100 GeV/\(c\).
* The parton is fragmented using PYTHIA6 u-quark fragmentation, or gluon fragmentation for systematic uncertainty study.
* 1 million events containing exactly one jet have been generated.
* Particle-level jet population is obtained by running the jet reconstruction algorithm on the charged tracks of the generated PYTHIA jets. The following criteria are then applied: the fiducial cut \(|\eta_{jet}|<1-R\), condition on the leading hadron momentum \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm lead}>p_{\rm T,lead}^{\rm min}\).
* simulating the effects of finite TPC reconstruction efficiency and the TPC momentum resolution (Eq. 6.3).
* Detector-level jets are reconstructed by running jet reconstruction algorithm on the propagated PYTHIA jets.
* **Jet Matching:** Corresponding particle- and detector-level jets are then matched together. Matching is done on the geometrical basis: For a given particle-level jet (PLJ1) a detector-level partner is found (DLJ1) which is closest in \(\eta\)-\(\phi\) space. The distance between the two jets is required to satisfy \(\sqrt{(\Delta\eta)^{2}-(\Delta\phi)^{2}}<R\). For thisdetector-level jet the closest particle-level jet (PLJ2) is found. If this jet corresponds to the original particle-level jet (PLJ1=PLJ2), both jets (PLJ1, DLJ1) are then matched together.
* Detector-level jet population is required to satisfy the same criteria as for real data analysis: \(|\eta_{jet}|<1-R\), \(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{lead}}>p_{\mathrm{T,lead}}^{\mathrm{min}}\), \(A>A_{\mathrm{min}}\).
* Particle-level jet population is required to satisfy: \(|\eta_{jet}|<1-R\), \(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{lead}}>p_{\mathrm{T,lead}}^{\mathrm{min}}\).
* For each matched jet pair a bin in the instrumental response matrix \(R_{\mathrm{instr}}\left[p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}},p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{ \mathrm{part}}\right]\) corresponding to \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}}\) and \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}}\) is incremented.
* Each bin of the response matrix is then scaled by \(1/n_{i}^{gen}\), where \(n_{i}^{gen}\) is the total number of generated particle level jets with momentum falling into the \(i^{th}\) bin on y-axis.

#### Jet Reconstruction Efficiency

When calculating the jet reconstruction efficiency, one should not compare directly detector-level and particle-level distributions bin-by-bin. Due to the detector effects, a jet having \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}}\) will be typically detected with \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}}\neq p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}}\) and thus potentially migrate from \(i\)-th \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) bin to \(j\)-th. Comparing \(i\)-th bin of particle-level spectrum with \(i\)-th bin of detector level spectrum is therefore not a good strategy and one has to take into account the forementioned bin migration.

Jet reconstruction efficiency is thus defined with respect to particle-level jets as

\[\varepsilon_{\mathrm{jet}}(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}})=\frac{\frac{ \mathrm{d}N_{\mathrm{jet}}}{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}}} \otimes\widetilde{\mathbf{R}^{-1}}\left[p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}} \to p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}}\right]}{\frac{\mathrm{d}N_{ \mathrm{jet}}}{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}}}}, \tag{6.9}\]

where \(\frac{\mathrm{d}N_{\mathrm{jet}}}{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{dete}}}\) and \(\frac{\mathrm{d}N_{\mathrm{jet}}}{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}^{\mathrm{part}}}\) are the detector-level and particle-level jet spectra. The expression in the numerator of the Eq. 6.9 is to be understood as unfolded and regularized detector-level spectrum. The jet reconstruction efficiency does not depend on \(R\) significantly. The fragmentation model and the tracking efficiency uncertainty (\(\pm\) 5% absolute) change the jet reconstruction efficiency within approx. 5% as can be seen on Fig. 6.16.

#### Jet Energy Resolution

The jet energy resolution (JER) is the degree of uncertainty to which we can measure the jet momentum due to the detector effects. It is dominated by the TPC tracking efficiency and it exhibits quite significant \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\)-dependence. The jet energy resolution is obtained in the same way as the detector response matrix - the same set of matched PYTHIA 6 particle level jets and detector level jets is used for this measurement.

